The React Compiler Just Made Your Memoization Knowledge Obsolete. Here’s What to Learn Instead.
React Compiler v1.0 didn’t just automate a task. It deprecated a decade of hard-won React expertise — and replaced it with a different set of questions entirely.
Press enter or click to view image in full size

Let me describe a type of React developer you’ve definitely met.
They know the render cycle cold. They can look at a component tree and tell you exactly which renders are unnecessary, which callbacks are being recreated on every tick, which derived values are being recomputed when they don’t need to be. They reach for useMemo, useCallback, and React.memo with the confidence of someone who learned the hard way — through sluggish UIs, through profiler sessions, through the specific frustration of watching a list re-render 60 times per second because a parent component passed a new object reference on every render.
That knowledge took years to build. It was legitimate expertise.
React Compiler v1.0, which shipped stable in October 2025, is more precise at memoization than they are. It catches cases they’d miss. It memoizes at a granularity that useMemo literally cannot reach — including values computed after an early conditional return, which a hook can't even touch. It does all of this at build time, with no runtime cost, and it has been running in production at Meta — including the Quest Store — for years before most developers heard of it.
The question isn’t whether to use the compiler. The answer to that is yes, immediately, for every new project. The question is: what does React expertise actually mean now? What’s left to know when the compiler handles the part that used to separate good React developers from great ones?
That’s a more interesting question than “how do I set it up” — and it’s the one nobody is answering.

What the Compiler Actually Does (Precisely, Not Vaguely)
Every tutorial says “it automatically memoizes your components.” That’s true but undersells the mechanism.
Before the compiler, React’s rendering model was pull-based and pessimistic: re-render everything, and it’s your job to opt out of unnecessary work. Every time a parent re-rendered, all its children re-rendered too — unless you’d manually wrapped values in useMemo, functions in useCallback, and components in React.memo. The API told you how to opt out, but not where. Finding the right places required profiling, intuition, and enough experience to recognize the patterns.
The compiler flips this at the source level. It reads your component as static code, tracks every variable, traces which values can change between renders and which cannot, and inserts memoization exactly where it’s needed — not where you think it’s needed. The result in your compiled output looks roughly like this:
// What you write
function ProductCard({ product, onAddToCart }) {
const discountedPrice = product.price * (1 - product.discount);
const handleClick = () => onAddToCart(product.id); return (
<div onClick={handleClick}>
<h2>{product.name}</h2>
<span>${discountedPrice.toFixed(2)}</span>
</div>
);
}// What the compiler produces (conceptually)
function ProductCard({ product, onAddToCart }) {
const $ = _cache(4); let discountedPrice;
if ($[0] !== product.price || $[1] !== product.discount) {
discountedPrice = product.price * (1 - product.discount);
$[0] = product.price;
$[1] = product.discount;
$[2] = discountedPrice;
} else {
discountedPrice = $[2];
}
// handleClick, JSX — same granular caching
}You write plain JavaScript. The compiler produces precisely memoized JavaScript. No dependency arrays. No referential equality juggling. No useMemo that wraps too much or too little.
The Meta Quest Store saw initial load improvements up to 12% and certain interactions become 2.5× faster — on a codebase that already had experienced engineers manually optimizing for years. The compiler found things they didn’t.

The Three Hooks That Just Became Escape Hatches
useMemo, useCallback, and React.memo still exist. They still work. The compiler doesn't remove them — it works around them, though with a catch worth knowing: if your manual memoization doesn't match what the compiler would have inferred, it bails out of optimizing that component entirely. Your manual hook becomes a ceiling, not a floor.
The mental model that needs to change: these hooks were defaults. Now they’re exceptions.
Every useMemo in your codebase in 2026 should have a documented reason. Not "this is expensive" — specific. Here are the three cases where the compiler genuinely can't help and manual memoization still earns its place:
1. Third-party libraries that depend on referential equality. The classic example is TanStack Table V8, where internal comparison logic assumes stable function references. The compiler can’t know what an external library does with a value — it only sees your code. If a chart library, canvas system, or any callback-by-reference API needs a stable reference that the compiler might not preserve, keep the hook. Document why.
// Keep this — TanStack Table V8 compares column defs by reference
const columns = useMemo(() => [
{ header: 'Name', accessorKey: 'name' },
// ...
], []); // Explicitly stable — compiler won't catch cross-library expectations2. Values inside try/catch blocks. The compiler can't analyze control flow that might throw. If your useMemo lives inside a try/catch — common in error-boundary-heavy code — the compiler skips it. Keep the hook there. This is a known limitation the React team has flagged for future work.
// Compiler skips this — keep useMemo
try {
const processed = useMemo(() => riskyTransform(data), [data]);
} catch (e) {
// ...
}3. Explicit useEffect dependency control. If a memoized value feeds into a useEffect dependency array and you need precise control over when the effect fires — not just when the value changes, but which change triggers it — manual memoization gives you that control. The compiler is more granular than you, but it's also automatic. When you need to reason explicitly about effect timing, own that explicitly.
Everything outside these three cases: let the compiler handle it. If you’re reaching for useMemo on derived state, formatted values, filtered arrays, or inline callbacks — that's habit, not necessity. Remove it and let the compiler do it more precisely than you would have.

The Migration You Should Actually Do
Don’t delete all your memoization in one PR. That’s how you create regressions nobody can diagnose on a Tuesday morning.
Step 1 — Enable the compiler first, ship nothing else.
npm install babel-plugin-react-compiler@latest eslint-plugin-react-hooks@7For Next.js 16:
// next.config.js
const nextConfig = {
reactCompiler: true,
};For Vite:
// vite.config.js
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import react from '@vitejs/plugin-react';export default defineConfig({
plugins: [
react({
babel: {
plugins: ['babel-plugin-react-compiler'],
},
}),
],
});Ship this. Verify in production. Check your performance metrics — Lighthouse, Core Web Vitals, whatever you track. If something breaks, use "use no memo" to exclude that component temporarily and file an issue:
function ProblematicComponent({ data }) {
"use no memo"; // TODO: investigate compiler interaction with ExternalLib
// ...
}Step 2 — Remove manual memoization incrementally, starting with the obvious.
Derived state — useMemo on filtered lists, sorted arrays, formatted values, calculations. These are the compiler's home turf. Remove them first. The compiler handles them more granularly than your hook did.
// Remove this
const sortedItems = useMemo(
() => [...items].sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name)),
[items]
);// Just write this
const sortedItems = [...items].sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));Step 3 — Audit what remains.
After the compiler is enabled and the easy memoization is gone, look at what’s left. Every surviving useMemo and useCallback should be in one of the three categories above. If it isn't, it's noise — remove it. If it is, add a comment explaining why. That comment is now load-bearing documentation.

What React Expertise Actually Looks Like Now
Here’s the honest answer to the question that matters.
The compiler took one specific skill off the table: the ability to manually optimize React’s render cycle through memoization. That skill was real. It mattered. For a decade it separated developers who understood React’s internals from those who didn’t.
What it didn’t take is everything else.
Understanding why components re-render — the fundamental model of React’s reactivity — still matters. The compiler optimizes inside that model; it doesn’t replace the model. A developer who doesn’t understand the render cycle can’t reason about the three cases where the compiler fails. They can’t diagnose the "use no memo" situations. They can't tell when an effect is firing too often because the compiler's memoization changed the reference the effect was watching.
Understanding the server/client boundary is now the harder problem. With React Server Components, the question isn’t “did this value memoize correctly?” — it’s “should this data even reach the client, or should it stay on the server?” The compiler has nothing to say about that. That’s architectural judgment, and no compiler replaces it.
Understanding composition — how to structure components so the compiler can see what it needs to see — is now an explicit skill. The compiler works best on components that follow the Rules of React cleanly: pure render functions, no mutations during render, no reading from mutable external state without proper subscriptions. Write messy components and the compiler bails out on them. Write clean ones and it makes them fast for free.
The developers who understood React deeply enough to optimize it manually will adapt faster than the ones who learned the patterns without the principles. The patterns are gone. The principles are what’s left.

The Broader Signal
React started as a UI library that made you think explicitly about when things render. That was the deal — you got predictability in exchange for writing your own optimization layer.
The compiler ends that deal. You no longer have to think about when things render, because the compiler thinks about it for you, more precisely than you could.
That’s not a loss of control. It’s a shift in what you control. You still decide what components exist, what data they receive, where the server boundary sits, how the state flows, which effects fire and when. The compiler handles the mechanical work of making those decisions fast.
A decade ago, a good React developer was someone who understood the render cycle well enough to optimize it. That’s still worth understanding — but it’s no longer the ceiling. The ceiling is now architecture: how you structure data flow, where you place boundaries, what you put on the server versus the client, how you design components that are easy for both humans and compilers to reason about.
The compiler didn’t make React easier. It made the easy problems automatic and surfaced the hard ones.

Setup in 60 Seconds
# Install
npm install babel-plugin-react-compiler@latest eslint-plugin-react-compiler# Verify it's working — look for the "Memo ✨" badge in React DevTools
# on components the compiler successfully optimizedCompatible with React 17 and up. Works with Next.js 16 (reactCompiler: true), Vite, Remix, and React Router v7. Expo SDK 54 ships with it enabled by default.
The ESLint plugin (eslint-plugin-react-compiler) catches Rules of React violations that would cause the compiler to bail out — run it before enabling the compiler in production so you know what you're working with.

